        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Measuring the floor and stopping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Railings, stairs and fascia are routinely half the stainable surface on a deck. Leave them out and you buy half the stain you need.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Trusting the top of the coverage range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The 400 square feet on the can is smooth new wood in ideal conditions. Weathered treated pine may deliver 150. Plan for the low end.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying cans on two different days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Different lot numbers dry to different shades. Buy everything at once, check the codes, and box the cans together before you start.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Ignoring the sprayer penalty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Spraying is fast and loses a quarter of the product to drift and gaps. If you spray, buy a quarter more and back-brush every board.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using the leftover to add a coat that is not wanted</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A one-coat product does not become better with a second coat; it becomes sticky. Store the spare for touch-ups instead.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a gallon of deck stain cover?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It depends on the product and the wood. A penetrating oil stain on rough or weathered wood covers roughly 150 to 250 square feet per gallon on the first coat. On smooth new wood the same product stretches to 250 to 400. Water-based semi-transparents run 250 to 350, and solid stains 300 to 400. Second coats go 30 to 50 per cent further because the wood is already partly sealed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How much stain do I need for a 12 by 16 deck?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The boards are 192 square feet, but with 36 feet of railing, four steps and the fascia the real surface is closer to 400 square feet. With a penetrating oil at 200 square feet per gallon on the first coat and 300 on the second, that is about 3.3 gallons, so buy 4 gallons or a 5-gallon pail once you add a 10 to 15 per cent margin.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you calculate deck stain for railings?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Measure the linear feet of railing and multiply by 3 to 4. A standard 36-inch railing with balusters on 4-inch centres has roughly 3 to 4 square feet of stainable surface per linear foot once you count both sides of every baluster, the top and bottom rails, the cap and the posts. A solid privacy-style rail is simply height times length times two.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy extra deck stain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, 10 to 15 per cent more than the formula gives you, and buy it all at once. Coverage rates on the can are best-case figures, weathered wood drinks more than you expect, and if you run out mid-job a can from a different batch can be a visibly different shade. Leftover stain in a sealed can is useful for touch-ups for two to three years.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does spraying deck stain use more product than brushing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, plan on 20 to 30 per cent more. Airless and HVLP sprayers lose product to overspray and to the gaps between boards, and they tend to lay stain on heavier than a pad or brush. A stain pad on a pole or a roller followed by back-brushing is the most efficient method per gallon.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 5-gallon pail of deck stain worth it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Usually, if your total comes to more than about 3.5 gallons. The per-gallon price is lower, the whole job comes from one batch so there is no colour shift, and there is no need to intermix cans. The downside is that a partly used pail is harder to store well than a sealed gallon can.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need two coats of deck stain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Read the can. Most penetrating oils and many water-based semi-transparents want a second coat applied wet-on-wet or within a short window. Some products, including many solid stains and one-coat formulas such as Ready Seal, are designed for a single application and a second coat will not dry properly. Budget accordingly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What if the wood is rough or very weathered?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Use the low end of the coverage range and expect the first coat to disappear into the wood. Rough-sawn lumber and old, sun-bleached boards can absorb twice as much as smooth new wood. If the deck has just been sanded or stripped it is also thirsty. When in doubt, buy the extra gallon; it is cheaper than a second trip and a mismatched batch.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

